				html: '\r\n          <h4>Discussion:</h4>\r\n          <SubHeading />\r\n          <LanguageSets />\r\n          <Paragraphs>\r\n            <p><div class="image-box">Image: WEB-0058_barnowl</div>As mentioned above, the terms barn owl, screech owl, and white owl are alternative names for the same owl. There is a long tradition among both Jewish and Christian scholars of translating <i>tinshemeth</i> as “barn owl” (<a>NAB</a>, also <a>NIV</a> with “white owl"). <a>NEB</a> and <a>REB</a> follow Driver’s suggestion “little owl", but this does not have as wide support among scholars as “barn owl,” which is also the modern Hebrew meaning of <i>tinshemeth</i>. The <a>KJV</a> and <a>RSV</a> renderings of “swan” and “water hen” respectively can be disregarded. Swans are extremely rare in the land of Israel, and “water hen” is too vague a term to be useful.</p>\r\n            <p>The word <i>tinshemeth</i> actually occurs three times in the Bible. Twice the word probably refers to the barn owl, but the third occurrence is a reference to a type of lizard or chameleon. (See <l target="FAUNA:4.3">4.3 Chameleon</l>.)</p>\r\n          </Paragraphs>\r\n        '
