			{
				type: 'translation',
				html: '\r\n          <h4>Translation:</h4>\r\n          <SubHeading />\r\n          <LanguageSets />\r\n          <Paragraphs>\r\n            <p>Since fennel is not well known, most translators will need to transliterate from a major language. Some possibilities are:</p>\r\n            <p>1. transliteration from Hebrew: <i>helibena</i>, <i>elbenahi</i>, <i>lebena</i>;</p>\r\n            <p>2. transliteration from Latin via English: <i>galbanum</i>;</p>\r\n            <p>3. substitution of a local type of gum, adding <i>chelbenah</i> or <i>galbanum</i> as a tag or in a footnote.</p>\r\n            <p>4. transliteration of the name of the plant with a classifier: gum of <i>ferula</i> (French), <i>feneli</i> (English), <i>hinojo</i> /<i>ferula</i> (Spanish).</p>\r\n          </Paragraphs>\r\n              <BibleImages>\r\n                <BibleImage Type="ILL" Id="WEB-0216_fennel">\r\n                  <Collection>WEB</Collection>\r\n                  <Path>marble-images-flora-fauna-realia\\FAUNA</Path>\r\n                  <FileName>WEB-0216_fennel.jpg</FileName>\r\n                  <Copyright>Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler\'s Medizinal-Pflanzen (Wikimedia Commons)</Copyright>\r\n                  <Definition Id="" LanguageCode="en"></Definition>\r\n                  <Caption>Fennel plant - botanical illustration</Caption>\r\n                  <Description />\r\n                </BibleImage>\r\n                <BibleImage Type="ILL" Id="WEB-0217_fennel_flower">\r\n                  <Collection>WEB</Collection>\r\n                  <Path>marble-images-flora-fauna-realia\\FAUNA</Path>\r\n                  <FileName>WEB-0217_fennel_flower.jpg</FileName>\r\n                  <Copyright>Tigerente (Wikimedia Commons)</Copyright>\r\n                  <Definition Id="" LanguageCode="en"></Definition>\r\n                  <Caption>Fennel flower</Caption>\r\n                  <Description />\r\n                </BibleImage>\r\n              </BibleImages>\r\n        '
			}
