				type: 'translation',
				html: '\r\n        <h4>Translation:</h4>\r\n        <SubHeading />\r\n        <LanguageSets />\r\n        <Paragraphs>\r\n          <p>The Hebrew word <i>sgor</i> looks like the imperative form of the verb meaning “close, shut off,” so the Septuagint understood it to mean “capture, make a prisoner.” Many translations take it to be a second weapon with the sword mentioned just before it, and most have rendered it “javelin” (<a>RSV</a>, <a>NJPSV</a>). However, <i>A Handbook on Psalms</i> (page 329) points out that there is evidence from Qumran that the <i>sgor</i> was a kind of axe used in war. Several translations (<a>GNT</a>, <a>NASB</a>, <a>ITCL</a>) have accepted this meaning. <a>NJB</a>, citing the Qumran information, has “pike,” which was a long thrusting spear. This Handbook recommends “battle-axe.”</p>\r\n        </Paragraphs>\r\n        <BibleImages>\r\n          <BibleImage id="WEB-0385_battle_axe">\r\n            <FileName>WEB-0385_battle_axe.jpg</FileName>\r\n            <Collection>WEB</Collection>\r\n            <Copyright>Jebulon, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons</Copyright>\r\n            <Caption>Double-headed battle axe</Caption>\r\n          </BibleImage>\r\n        </BibleImages>\r\n      '
