				type: 'translation',
				html: '\r\n        <h4>Translation:</h4>\r\n        <SubHeading />\r\n        <LanguageSets />\r\n        <Paragraphs>\r\n          <p>The Hebrew word <i>reshef</i> normally refers to something fiery (for example, spark or lightning bolt; see <s>H01800500700016</s>; <s>H01907804800022</s>; <s>H02200800600046</s>). In <s>H01907600400006</s> it is parallel with the word for “bow” and probably indicates some kind of burning arrow.</p>\r\n          <p>\r\n            <s>H02500301300008</s>: See the comments at <l>2.14.3 Quiver&lt;REALIA:2.14.3&gt;</l>.</p>\r\n          <p>The Hebrew word <i>mateh</i> usually refers to a rod, club, or shepherd’s staff (see <l>1.2.4 Rod, club, shepherd’s staff&lt;REALIA:1.2.4&gt;</l>), but in <s>H03500300900010</s> many translations render it “arrows” (<a>RSV</a>, <a>GNT</a>; supported by <a>HOTTP</a> and <i>A Handbook on The Books of Nahum, Habakkuk, and Zephaniah</i>). This verse is very unclear in Hebrew.</p>\r\n        </Paragraphs>\r\n        <BibleImages>\r\n          <BibleImage id="UBS-0015_arrow">\r\n            <FileName>UBS-0015_arrow.jpg</FileName>\r\n            <Collection>UBS</Collection>\r\n            <Copyright>© Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft, Stuttgart by United Bible Societies</Copyright>\r\n            <Caption>Arrow</Caption>\r\n          </BibleImage>\r\n        </BibleImages>\r\n      '
