				html: '\r\n        <h4>Translation:</h4>\r\n        <SubHeading />\r\n        <LanguageSets />\r\n        <Paragraphs>\r\n          <p>The Hebrew expression <i>kaf man‘ul</i> in <s>H02200500500032</s> probably refers to a wooden protrusion or a cord used to pull back the door bar to allow the door to open. <a>GNT</a> has “handle of the door,” while others say “handles of the bolt” (<a>RSV</a>, <a>NASB</a>) or “handles of the lock” (<a>KJV</a>). <a>CEV</a> has simply “door.”</p>\r\n          <p>\r\n            <image id="OAI-0081_door_locked_with_bar.png" />The unusual Greek word used in <s>L07300101800000</s> can mean either a lock (so <a>RSV</a>) or a bar drawn across a door to secure it (so <a>GNT</a>).</p>\r\n        </Paragraphs>\r\n        <BibleImages>\r\n          <BibleImage id="WEB-0403_lock_en">\r\n            <FileName>WEB-0403_lock_en.jpg</FileName>\r\n            <Collection>WEB</Collection>\r\n            <Copyright>© Willh26, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons</Copyright>\r\n            <Caption>Diagram of an ancient Egyptian lock mechanism for a door</Caption>\r\n          </BibleImage>\r\n          <BibleImage id="OAI-0081_door_locked_with_bar.png">\r\n            <FileName>OAI-0081_door_locked_with_bar.png</FileName>\r\n            <Collection>OPEN-AI</Collection>\r\n            <Copyright>Image generated by ChatGPT using OpenAI technology</Copyright>\r\n            <Caption>The door bar was an effective way of keeping out intruders</Caption>\r\n          </BibleImage>\r\n        </BibleImages>\r\n      '
