		title: 'Parapet, retaining wall',
		category: 'Realia',
		content:
			'<div class="section-box"><div class="section-label">reference</div>\r\n        <h4>Reference:</h4>\r\n        <SubHeading />\r\n        <LanguageSets>\r\n          <div class="lang-set"><strong>Hebrew</strong>\r\n            <em>מַעֲקֶה</em> \r\n            [ma‘aqeh] \r\n            <div class="ref-list">\r\n              <Reference>H00502200800014</Reference>\r\n            </div>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </LanguageSets>\r\n      </div>\n<div class="section-box"><div class="section-label">description and usage</div>\r\n        <h4>Description and usage:</h4>\r\n        <SubHeading />\r\n        <LanguageSets />\r\n        <Paragraphs>\r\n          <p>The parapet was a low stone wall built around the flat roof of a house to prevent people or things from falling off. See the illustration at <l>3.1 House, permanent dwelling&lt;REALIA:3.1&gt;</l>.</p>\r\n          <p>———</p>\r\n        </Paragraphs>\r\n      </div>\n<div class="section-box"><div class="section-label">translation</div>\r\n        <h4>Translation:</h4>\r\n        <SubHeading />\r\n        <LanguageSets />\r\n        <Paragraphs>\r\n          <p>Most translations use a single word to render the Hebrew word <i>ma‘aqeh</i>, for example, “parapet” (<a>RSV</a>) and “railing” (<a>GNT</a>). <a>NCV</a> and <a>CEV</a> have “low wall,” while <a>SPCL</a> “protective wall” is also good. The context of <s>H00502200800014</s> makes the purpose of the parapet clear as seen in the <a>GNT</a> rendering of this verse: “When you build a new house, be sure to put a railing around the edge of the roof. Then you will not be responsible if someone falls off and is killed.” Where possible and necessary, it is advisable to add a note explaining the nature and uses of the roof of an Israelite house (see <l>3.1.5 Roof, housetop&lt;REALIA:3.1.5&gt;</l>).</p>\r\n        </Paragraphs>\r\n      </div>',
