		category: 'Realia',
		content:
			'<div class="section-box"><div class="section-label">references</div>\r\n        <h4>References:</h4>\r\n        <LanguageSets>\r\n          <div class="lang-set"><strong>Hebrew</strong>\r\n            <em>מַזְלֵג</em> \r\n            [mazleg] \r\n            <div class="ref-list">\r\n              <Reference>H00900201300046</Reference>\r\n              <Reference>H00900201400044</Reference>\r\n            </div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class="lang-set"><strong>Hebrew</strong>\r\n            <em>מַזְלֵג</em> \r\n            [mizlgoth] \r\n            <div class="ref-list">\r\n              <Reference>H00202700300022</Reference>\r\n              <Reference>H00203800300042</Reference>\r\n              <Reference>H00400401400032</Reference>\r\n              <Reference>H01302801700006</Reference>\r\n              <Reference>H01400401600024</Reference>\r\n            </div>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </LanguageSets>\r\n      </div>\n<div class="section-box"><div class="section-label">description and usage</div>\r\n        <h4>Description and usage:</h4>\r\n        <SubHeading />\r\n        <LanguageSets />\r\n        <Paragraphs>\r\n          <p>\r\n            <image id="WEB-0451_fork.jpg" />The fork was used in the Tabernacle and Temple to turn sacrificial victims on the altar or to remove them. Its exact form is unknown, but it may have resembled a hook or a kind of two-pronged fork.</p>\r\n          <p>———</p>\r\n        </Paragraphs>\r\n      </div>\n<div class="section-box"><div class="section-label">translation</div>\r\n        <h4>Translation:</h4>\r\n        <SubHeading />\r\n        <LanguageSets />\r\n        <Paragraphs>\r\n          <p>For the Hebrew word <i>mizlgoth</i>, some translations (<a>RSV</a>, <a>REB</a>) have “forks,” while <a>GNT</a> says “hooks.” However, a more descriptive expression is recommended, such as “meat forks” (<a>NIV</a>, <a>CEV</a>, <a>FRCL</a>).</p>\r\n          <p>The Hebrew word <i>mazleg</i> for fork used to describe the actions of the sons of Eli in <s>H00900201300046 H00900201400044</s> differs slightly from the word <i>mizlgoth</i>. It is reasonable to assume that they used something ready at hand, probably the same fork used at the altar. Translators are advised to use the same word. In many languages the word used for both of these will be the same as the word for a common fork, used as an item of table cutlery.</p>\r\n        </Paragraphs>\r\n        <BibleImages>\r\n          <BibleImage id="WEB-0451_fork">\r\n            <FileName>WEB-0451_fork.jpg</FileName>\r\n            <Collection>WEB</Collection>\r\n            <Copyright>Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons</Copyright>\r\n            <Caption>Copper alloy fork, late 3rd–early 2nd millennium BCE, central Asia</Caption>\r\n          </BibleImage>\r\n        </BibleImages>\r\n      </div>',
