			{
				type: 'translation',
				html: '\r\n        <h4>Translation:</h4>\r\n        <SubHeading />\r\n        <LanguageSets />\r\n        <Paragraphs>\r\n          <p>In some languages the closest equivalent for “oil lamp” is “kerosene lamp.” Translators should avoid an expression that suggests a flashlight or any sort of electric light, which would be highly anachronistic. It is possible to say “lantern” or “light.” Another good cultural equivalent could be the candle, which works on the same principle as the oil lamp, only with solid fuel rather than oil. Candles were known in the ancient world, although they are not mentioned in the Bible.</p>\r\n          <p>As an object whose purpose is to give light, the lamp is sometimes used figuratively in Scripture. All the occurrences of the Hebrew word <i>nir</i> and the Greek word <i>lucerna</i> are figurative. The Hebrew word <i>ner</i> is used figuratively of Israel’s king (<s>H01002101700064</s>), God or some something about him, such as his protection, guidance, blessing or word (<s>H01002202900006</s>; <s>H01802101700006</s>; <s>H01901802900008</s>; <s>H01911910500002</s>; <s>H02000602300004</s>), life or death as the extinguishing of life (<s>H01801800600012</s>; <s>H01802101700006</s>; <s>H01913201700014</s>; <s>H02001300900010</s>; <s>H02002002000012</s>; <s>H02002402000016</s>), the spirit of man (<s>H02002002700002</s>), peace or normalcy (<s>H02402501000036</s>), and thoroughness (<s>H03600101200026</s>). The Greek word <i>luchnos</i> is figurative of a person’s enlightenment (<s>G04000602200004</s>; <s>G04201103400004</s>, <s>G04201103600038</s>), preparedness (<s>G04201203500016</s>), prophetic voice (<s>G04300503500008</s>), and a person’s face (<s>L07102601700002</s>).</p>\r\n          <p>“Lamp” is often used metaphorically for a person’s life; for example, at <s>H01802101700006</s> (<a>RSV</a> “How often is it that the lamp of the wicked is put out?”) <i>A Handbook on The Book of Job</i> (page 398) points out that this line speaks of premature or unexpected death.</p>\r\n          <p>In several places the light-giving element of the lamp becomes a metaphor for God’s direction, usually supplied by his word, as a person walks through life. An example is <s>H01911910500000</s>, which <a>NIV</a> renders “Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light for my path.”</p>\r\n          <p>While translators will usually agree that a particular passage is figurative, they may not always agree concerning the exact nature of the figure; for example, <a>GNT</a> and <a>GECL</a> do not agree about the meaning of the lamp imagery in <s>H01802900300006</s>. This line is literally “when his lamp shone upon my head” (<a>RSV</a>). <a>GNT</a> says “God was always with me then,” while <a>GECL</a> has “He gave me success every day.”</p>\r\n          <p>For <s>H02402501000036</s> see the comments at <l>5.10 Millstones, mill&lt;REALIA:5.10&gt;</l>.</p>\r\n          <p>When translating the Hebrew word <i>pishtah</i> in <s>H02304200300012</s>, more formal translations use the literal rendering “wick”; for example, for the whole line <a>RSV</a> has “and a dimly burning wick he will not quench.” Common-language translations usually avoid such a word-for-word rendering that will not be commonly understood. Several good examples are <a>GNT</a> “He will not … put out a flickering lamp,” <a>CEV</a> “He won’t … put out a dying flame,” and <a>NCV</a> “He will not … put out even a weak flame.” Where a translator prefers to retain the image of the wick, it may be rendered “small strip of cloth.”</p>\r\n        </Paragraphs>\r\n        <BibleImages>\r\n          <BibleImage id="WEB-0940_oil_lamps">\r\n            <FileName>WEB-0940_oil_lamps.jpg</FileName>\r\n            <Collection>WEB</Collection>\r\n            <Copyright>© Combirom2 Wikimedia Commons</Copyright>\r\n            <Caption>Oil lamps, Roman period</Caption>\r\n          </BibleImage>\r\n          <BibleImage id="WEB-0472_lamp">\r\n            <FileName>WEB-0472_lamp.jpg</FileName>\r\n            <Collection>WEB</Collection>\r\n            <Copyright>© Davidbena, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons</Copyright>\r\n            <Caption>Oil lamp, Roman period</Caption>\r\n          </BibleImage>\r\n        </BibleImages>\r\n      '
			}
