				html: '\r\n        <h4>Translation:</h4>\r\n        <SubHeading />\r\n        <LanguageSets />\r\n        <Paragraphs>\r\n          <p>Where a word is lacking in a language for “veil,” a descriptive phrase may be used, such as “thin cloth to hide the face.” It may be necessary to expand the translation a little to convey the meaning; compare <a>NCV</a> at <s>H00103801400022</s>, where it renders the second clause as “covered her face with a veil to hide who she was.”</p>\r\n          <p>Translators should be aware that it is only by historical accident that, in older English translations, the word “veil” was used also for the curtain that divided the Holy Place from the Most Holy Place (see <l>3.14.1.6 Curtain, veil, drape&lt;REALIA:3.14.1.6&gt;</l>). No attempt should be made to render them identically in the receptor language.</p>\r\n          <p>\r\n            <s>H02601301800036 H02601302100008</s>: The Hebrew word <i>mispachah</i> refers to a long veil, almost a sheath, reaching from the head to the feet.</p>\r\n          <p>The Hebrew word <i>rdid</i> in <s>H02200500700026</s> and <s>H02300302300024</s> probably refers to a long veil, covering more than just the face. Both the <i>mispachah</i> and the <i>rdid</i> were similar to the <i>niqab</i> or <i>burqah</i> worn by some Muslim women.</p>\r\n        </Paragraphs>\r\n        <BibleImages>\r\n          <BibleImage id="WEB-0506_veil">\r\n            <FileName>WEB-0506_veil.jpg</FileName>\r\n            <Collection>WEB</Collection>\r\n            <Copyright>© Trjames, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons</Copyright>\r\n            <Caption>Wedding dress and veil (Beit Jebrin, northwest of Hebron)</Caption>\r\n          </BibleImage>\r\n        </BibleImages>\r\n      '
